  "summary": "Persian micro-mosaic of star-patterned inlay built from hair-thin strips of wood, bone and wire.",
  "description": "Khatam-kari builds geometric stars from glued bundles — maple, jujube, walnut, brass and silver wire, bone — sliced crosswise into thin tiles that tessellate into dense six- and eight-pointed star fields. Safavid workshops brought the technique to its height for doors, Qur'an stands, mirror cases and boxes; Shiraz and Isfahan ateliers still cover jewelry cases, chessboards and gift objects. The best khatam reads as textile: a shimmering galaxy of pinpoint geometry under a glass-smooth surface.",
